1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,592
This is STAT 331, video 10 b. We solve the exam problems on prediction.

2
00:00:07,042 --> 00:00:20,930
We solve Tutorial 2 question 1, parts e and f. Then the two practice questions of Lecture 4. Then Assignment 1 question 3, with the berries data. Video 10 a gave the theory.

3
00:00:21,380 --> 00:00:36,505
Tutorial 2, question 1. There are 25 patients. The response is satisfaction. The covariate is the severity of the illness. The question gives the R output of the fit. We read only the numbers we need.

4
00:00:36,955 --> 00:00:49,713
The Estimate column gives the fitted line. Beta 0 hat is 179.1312. Beta 1 hat is minus 2.3283.

5
00:00:50,163 --> 00:01:01,533
The residual standard error is sigma hat. It is 13.52, on 23 degrees of freedom. That is n minus 2, because n is 25.

6
00:01:01,983 --> 00:01:18,645
The question also gives the mean of severity, 50.48, and the sum of squares, S x x, equal to 532.24. Now we have every number for the intervals. Pause the video and try parts e and f.

7
00:01:19,095 --> 00:01:40,065
Step 1. The predicted value is beta 0 hat plus beta 1 hat times 55. 179.1312 minus 2.3283 times 55 is 51.0747. This is also the estimated mean from part d.

8
00:01:40,515 --> 00:02:03,001
Step 2. Compute the x part. 1 over n is 1 over 25, which is 0.04. 55 minus 50.48 is 4.52. Its square is 20.4304. Divide by 532.24. The sum is 0.07839.

9
00:02:03,451 --> 00:02:24,933
Step 3. For a prediction, add 1. Sigma hat times the square root of 1.07839 is 13.52 times 1.03845. That is 14.04. The t quantile with 23 degrees of freedom is 2.069.

10
00:02:25,383 --> 00:02:53,714
Step 4. The half-width is 2.069 times 14.04, which is 29.04. So the 95 percent prediction interval is 22.03 to 80.12. The confidence interval for the mean, from part d, is 43.24 to 58.91. The prediction interval is much wider, because one new patient also has the error epsilon new.

11
00:02:54,164 --> 00:03:18,399
Part f. We do not have the 25 data points. But S x x gives the sample standard deviation of severity. s x is the square root of 532.24 over 24. That is 4.71. The purple region is x bar plus or minus 2 s x. Most of the severity values are in a region like this.

12
00:03:18,849 --> 00:03:28,662
At severity 55, we are inside this region. The green bar is the confidence interval, and the orange bar is the prediction interval.

13
00:03:29,112 --> 00:03:45,667
Severity 100 is 10.5 standard deviations above x bar. The formula still gives a number: y hat is minus 53.7. The prediction interval is minus 120.2 to 12.8.

14
00:03:46,117 --> 00:04:02,565
But we should not trust it. This is extrapolation. We have no data near 100. So we cannot check that the relation is linear there, or that the variance is the same. The interval formula assumes the model is correct at x new.

15
00:04:03,015 --> 00:04:08,199
Lecture 4, practice question 1. Pause the video and try parts a and b.

16
00:04:08,649 --> 00:04:32,009
Part a. The sample variance of x is s x squared. It equals S x x over n minus 1. So S x x equals n minus 1 times s x squared. Then beta 1 hat is S x y over n minus 1 times s x squared. Beta 0 hat is y bar minus beta 1 hat times x bar.

17
00:04:32,459 --> 00:04:41,568
Under the normal model, the MLEs of beta 0 and beta 1 are the least squares estimates. Video 5 showed this.

18
00:04:42,018 --> 00:04:52,258
Part b. The two estimators of sigma squared use the same S S E. Sigma hat squared divides by n minus 2. Here that is 18.

19
00:04:52,708 --> 00:04:57,913
The MLE divides by n, which is 20. So the MLE is smaller.

20
00:04:58,363 --> 00:05:12,102
The ratio of the variances is 20 over 18. Take the square root. Sigma hat over sigma hat MLE is the square root of 20 over 18. That is 1.054.

21
00:05:12,552 --> 00:05:23,069
Part c. We have n, S x x, x bar, S x y, and the sum of x i y i. We do not have y bar. Pause the video and try it.

22
00:05:23,519 --> 00:05:48,138
Use the short form of S x y. S x y equals the sum of x i y i, minus n x bar y bar. Solve for y bar. y bar is 3378 minus 353, over 50 times 4.8. That is 3025 over 240, which is 12.6042.

23
00:05:48,588 --> 00:05:58,892
Beta 1 hat is S x y over S x x. 353 over 180 is 1.9611.

24
00:05:59,342 --> 00:06:19,822
Beta 0 hat is y bar minus beta 1 hat x bar. 12.6042 minus 1.9611 times 4.8 is 3.1908. The chain shows the order: first y bar, then beta 1 hat, then beta 0 hat.

25
00:06:20,272 --> 00:06:39,045
Practice question 2. The degrees of freedom are n minus 2, which is 8. The question gives three probabilities for t with 8 degrees of freedom. First decide which quantile each one gives. P of T greater than minus 1.86 is 0.95.

26
00:06:39,495 --> 00:06:55,090
The t density is symmetric. So P of T greater than 1.86 is 0.05. So 1.86 is t 0.95, 8. A 90 percent interval needs this quantile.

27
00:06:55,540 --> 00:07:09,897
P of T less than 2.31 is 0.975. So 2.31 is t 0.975, 8. A 95 percent interval needs this quantile.

28
00:07:10,347 --> 00:07:27,009
P of the absolute value of T greater than 2.75 is 0.025. That is a 97.5 percent interval. No part of the question needs it. Pause the video and try parts a, b and c.

29
00:07:27,459 --> 00:07:41,773
Part a. The standard error of beta 1 hat is sigma hat over the square root of S x x. 0.4 over the square root of 16 is 0.4 over 4, which is 0.1.

30
00:07:42,223 --> 00:07:58,778
For 95 percent, use t 0.975, 8, which is 2.31. So the interval is 5 plus or minus 0.231. That is 4.769 to 5.231.

31
00:07:59,228 --> 00:08:19,729
Part b. The standard error of beta 0 hat is sigma hat times the square root of 1 over n, plus x bar squared over S x x. x bar is 0, so the second term is 0. 0.4 times the square root of 0.1 is 0.1265.

32
00:08:20,179 --> 00:08:37,374
The interval is minus 2 plus or minus 2.31 times 0.1265. That is minus 2 plus or minus 0.292, so minus 2.292 to minus 1.708.

33
00:08:37,824 --> 00:08:44,032
Part c. The predicted value is minus 2 plus 5 times 5. That is 23.

34
00:08:44,482 --> 00:09:09,165
The standard error for a prediction is sigma hat times the square root of 1 plus 1 over n plus x new minus x bar squared over S x x. Here that is 1 plus 0.1 plus 25 over 16, which is 2.6625. The square root is 1.632, and 0.4 times that is 0.6527.

35
00:09:09,615 --> 00:09:20,879
A 90 percent interval has alpha equal to 0.1. So we need t 0.95, 8. From the first probability, it is 1.86.

36
00:09:21,329 --> 00:09:42,022
The half-width is 1.86 times 0.6527, which is 1.214. So the 90 percent prediction interval is 21.786 to 24.214. Do not use 2.31 here. That quantile is for 95 percent.

37
00:09:42,472 --> 00:10:04,723
Assignment 1, question 3. There are 90 berries. x is the sugar content in grams per litre. y is the chewiness in millijoules. Part a is the scatterplot. The sugar values take only 6 levels, from 176.5 to 258.5. Pause the video and try the question.

38
00:10:05,173 --> 00:10:20,405
Part b. We compute the least squares estimates from the data. Beta 0 hat is 7.6629. Beta 1 hat is minus 0.02280. The green line is the fitted line.

39
00:10:20,855 --> 00:10:32,012
Interpretation of beta 1 hat. For each additional gram per litre of sugar, the estimated mean chewiness is 0.0228 millijoules lower.

40
00:10:32,462 --> 00:10:47,993
Interpretation of beta 0 hat. It is the estimated mean chewiness of berries with 0 grams per litre of sugar. No berry in the data has sugar near 0. So this number has no useful meaning by itself.

41
00:10:48,443 --> 00:11:12,848
Part c. First the standard error. Sigma hat is 0.9178, and S x x is 70657.4. The standard error of beta 1 hat is 0.9178 over the square root of 70657.4. That is 0.003453.

42
00:11:13,298 --> 00:11:27,805
The t statistic is beta 1 hat minus 0, over its standard error. Minus 0.02280 over 0.003453 is minus 6.60.

43
00:11:28,255 --> 00:11:49,737
The test is two-sided, with 88 degrees of freedom. The p-value is 2 times the probability that t 88 is above 6.60. That is 3.0 times 10 to the minus 9. On the plot, t is far beyond the critical values, plus or minus 1.987.

44
00:11:50,187 --> 00:12:04,587
The p-value is less than 0.05, so we reject H 0 at the 5 percent level. The data give strong evidence of a linear association between sugar content and mean chewiness.

45
00:12:05,037 --> 00:12:52,797
Part d. At 220 grams per litre, the estimated mean chewiness is 7.6629 minus 0.02280 times 220. That is 2.6475. The standard error is sigma hat times the square root of 1 over 90 plus 220 minus 217.33 squared over S x x. That is 0.0972. With t 0.975, 88 equal to 1.987, the 95 percent confidence interval is 2.454 to 2.841. It is narrow, because 220 is near x bar.

46
00:12:53,247 --> 00:13:14,473
Part e. At 110 grams per litre, the predicted chewiness is 5.155. For a prediction, add 1 under the square root. The standard error is 0.9945. The 95 percent prediction interval is 3.179 to 7.132.

47
00:13:14,923 --> 00:13:35,019
Why mistrust this interval? 110 is far below the smallest sugar value in the data, 176.5. The red region has no data. We cannot check the linear model, or the constant variance, there. This is extrapolation, as in Tutorial 2 part f.

48
00:13:35,469 --> 00:13:47,352
To recap. Read beta 0 hat, beta 1 hat, sigma hat, and the degrees of freedom from the output. Then the prediction interval needs x bar and S x x.

49
00:13:47,802 --> 00:14:06,255
Pick the t quantile from the level. A 90 percent interval uses t 0.95. A 95 percent interval uses t 0.975. Use the short forms for S x x and S x y when the question gives summary numbers.

50
00:14:06,705 --> 00:14:17,116
An x value far outside the data gives an interval, but we should not trust it. The next video starts the matrix review for multiple linear regression.
